I let the trickle of information roll into my mind, the Skills letting me know of Zeon's pre-war military syllabus. 

*Not exactly the Black Tri-stars, but at least i can dig up what a battalion should be composed of, I guess, as well as the normal disposition of an academy troup.*

With the realization that I would need to wait for help, I settled in to trying to compose a song in my head. Thanks, Basara-san. 

The flash of what looked like a directed spotlight suddenly caught my eye. 

*And I was just getting comfortable too...*

---

The Briarpatch Brigade was all assembled near a porthole close to the side facing the wreckage. After sitting through some inner ear twisting maneuvers, Mitchell had asked a passing crewman what the commotion was.

"SOS sir, looks like some kinda misjump. Can't say. Cap'n says to heave to and see if there's anything to salvage."

Mitchell had just nodded, then in the zero G conditions made to find an observation site. That his lancemates followed him probably spoke for their own curiosity than any inherent leadership skill he owned.

"Looks like one of them picked up something." Andy muttered.

Sure enough, Mitchell could trace the tether line connecting the spacehand that was outside to the ship, to a point in space. It looked like he was hauling a pod of some sort, something that could plausibly fit a man inside it.

Watching a man painstakingly use his EVA tool to push what looked like a quarter ton of metal into the exposed cargo bay was not the best use of anyone's time. Mitchell, adn the rest of his team, kept looking on.

"Whaddya think it is?", said Margrette.

"Hopefully not a nuke", said Lillia.

"Maybe it'll be a cache of diamonds?" hoped Mitchell.

Lillia just stared at him. He shrugged.

Maybe best to go back to quarters and sort out the next job.

---

When the space-suited man rapped on my little window I almost panicked, thinking I was about to get spaced. Thankfully he realized the pod was occupied, and made to bring my pod into his ship.

I was both panicking and excited. Excited, because I would be talking to real astronaut types! Panicking, because I was afraid I'd spill the beans on the magic nonsense floating in my head --

```
[** The Gacha System is not Nonsense **]
```

*Okay, thank you Gacha*

--spill the beans on the miracle cornucopia in my head. I figured it was going to be tuned mostly to make me stand heads above any others but it would be work. 

*Maybe I need a mindset change.*

As I debated this, I felt the bump of something contacting my pod, and I could see lighting. Moments later, the light changed a little, showing more details. Honestly it looked a bit like a metal foundry crossed with a warehouse from what little I saw, with my pods perspective seemingly pointed at the ceiling. 

Just then the light saying EXTERNAL ATMO blinked from red to green. Before I could process that, my pod suddenly hissed, as its sides snapped open letting the door in front of my face swing away. 

My lungs were breathing in spaceship air! It smelled a bit like a musty garage or toilet, but real spaceship air was real spaceship air. I levered myself up. Around me were very human, very grungy looking faces wearing assorted coveralls, except for one person who had a futuristic looking spacesuit on. They were next to my pod, and they spoke with a feminine voice.

"Hey pal, you're OK. We've got some medics coming down."

"Sure" I said. "Appreciate the help."

The figure nodded before walking away and releasing their helmet from their suit. They didn't seem to step, rather glid across the floor. I noticed they were floating to a pair of lift doors that opened up. More people streamed out, folks wearing cloth masks, neoprene gloves and carrying a bag with the classic caduceus logo on it. 

As they got to me and started going over my vitals, I suddenly noticed, as my head was rotated to the side, that I was sharing space with an honest to god giant robot.

*Where am I...*

I didn't notice until a little later, when I was in the ship's infirmary, that I did not have a single stitch of clothing on me.

---
Captain Paolo of the Union-class ship, "Wants-No-Nails" was annoyed. His schedule was tight enough, and having to beg the jump ship at the local star's zenith for more time grated at his pride. 

Spacers looked down on people who ignored people in distress, though, so he bit down on that feeling. He looked to his XO, Schiavo. Schaivo's own frail form was taut, as if expecting to swallow a bitter pill.

"Well, ex-oh. Who is our guest, and what was he doing in that pod?"

The XO cleared his throat. Crewmates at their station looked at their consoles as if there was nothing else in the world for their eyes. One of them surreptiously pulled away his headset. 

Schiavo noticed but said nothing about it. What he did say was, "He identifies himself as an, ah, information technology specialist from the civilian world. Name of Karl, no last name given. When we asked him about what his port of origin was he said...well he said Earth, sir."

Paolo looked at him with an eye cocked. "Earth? He means Terra? That's a long way out from here, isn't it!"

"Yes sir, maybe a year's travel given prevailing jumpship trade routes. Perhaps a coordinated circuit?"

A grumble. Something didn't sit right with Paolo on this, but he didn't have time to play detective.

"He didn't say why he was here, did he?"

"No, captain. The survivor seems to not remember ever boarding a ship at all. Insists the last thing he remembered was being at home."

"Strange things. He doesn't seem like a spy for any of the Houses. He does look a bit odd though."

"Yes sir," agreed Schiavo. "The doctor seems to think he's probably from the Terran Belter community. Lifelong spacers and all, which explains the frail look."

"Frail with a potbelly, hah! Anyway, we'll deposit him at the next port of call together with the mercs. We'll let the JumpShip master know why we had to delay later, after this jump."

Schiavo nodded. The safe route was the best for this, after all. Let the man call home on his own.

Back to the daily grind.

---

I was lying in the little cot and the cotton overalls they provided me when they announced they were docking to a JumpShip. At that point I had a pretty decent inkling where I was, although I didn't pick up any dates yet. Somewhere in BattleTech, was my best guess. 

The big 'Mech in the cargo hold, with its vaguely catlike head seemed to have been a Panther with no right arm. I didn't catch any other telltales as I was carted into the elevator to the medical bay so quickly. 

At least the machines I was hooked up to stayed close to the ideals of the Future Of the Eighties that exemplified BT. Lots of bakelite buttons, the EKG was a vector display of some sort. The IV bag I was hooked up to seemed pretty normal though, with perhaps some extra bits and bobs to cater for zero G stuff.

Compare that to the stuff in my various memories. Basara had mostly his musical talent, and his skill as a Valkyrie pilot. I was pretty sure that would translate well to flying the Hornet as well as driving the Sutherland. Zenna's skills seemed more basic, all to do with administration and managing operations in a military unit, if rather textbook. 

Whatever a Starjack was, it seemed to sync up well with space living. The lack of muscle mass meant I was going to have problems in Earth-standard gravities, so that meant a bunch of exercise I didn't really want to do. 

Apprentice Math meant that given pen and paper I could pretty much Wolfram Alpha my way through calculus, given time. It would be slow going though, so I didn't think being a math tutor would be a good use of my skills. It could have parallels with my own training at home as a programmer and cluster admin, but I'd need to do some reading. 

Magpie's Eye might be real nice to spot treasure, but it didn't seem like it was picking anything up here. I had an inkling it would trigger when I was actively hunting for stuff to pawn or hoard.

Behind all that, Nimble and Speed was just seasoning on the melange that was my current setup. The real heavy hitters were my Vehicles.

I needed to find time and privacy to unpack those. I needed a way to launder my machines, so that I didn't pick up any curious snoops. A starfighter that had fake non-Newtonian handling via Bussard Ramscoops, plus a mini-mech with a Float System and an energy shield would mean many, many people after their secrets.

I mentally noted down my goals, and the Gacha system quite helpfully threw up a journal page.

```
[[**GOALS**]]

1. Find a way to transport my vehicles around the Inner Sphere
2. Find partners to leverage my skills and gain some profit
3. Fix the Inner Sphere.
```


Tall order for a single man, who before this had never even dreamt of going outstation for a job. Still, maybe I *did* have the capacity for the role...

